1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:09,160
That was the CTO's second question in
the last video, about work lost in a Git

2
00:00:09,200 --> 00:00:14,040
repository. The common answer in engineering
chat is "relax, Git keeps everything

3
00:00:14,080 --> 00:00:18,379
for 30 days, plus two weeks". Three
things are wrong with that sentence.

4
00:00:18,719 --> 00:00:22,973
Nothing happens when the time is up.
Expiry is work done by a command.

5
00:00:23,013 --> 00:00:28,065
The two weeks aren't added on top of the
30 days. And there are situations in which

6
00:00:28,105 --> 00:00:33,096
there was never a reflog entry to begin
with: a deleted branch, a server, a tag, a

7
00:00:33,136 --> 00:00:38,249
branch you only fetched. The reflog, remember,
is Git's local log of where each name

8
00:00:38,289 --> 00:00:38,985
has pointed.

9
00:00:39,325 --> 00:00:43,433
If you give the CTO the comfortable
number and the work was in one of those

10
00:00:43,473 --> 00:00:46,363
situations, you'll be wrong
at the moment it matters.

11
00:00:46,403 --> 00:00:50,234
Keep that comfortable sentence in
mind. By the end you can correct it.

12
00:00:52,074 --> 00:00:55,909
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

13
00:00:55,949 --> 00:01:00,990
Last time you learned the four layers that
protect a commit, a saved snapshot of

14
00:01:01,030 --> 00:01:05,309
your project: refs, reflogs, a grace
period, and other repositories.

15
00:01:05,349 --> 00:01:07,659
And you learned how to read a reflog.

16
00:01:07,999 --> 00:01:12,776
Today you learn how long layers two and
three last, and what shortens them.

17
00:01:12,816 --> 00:01:17,979
Then two instruments. ORIG_HEAD, which is
convenient and holds exactly one value.

18
00:01:18,019 --> 00:01:23,245
And git fsck, which you met as an integrity
check and now use as a search tool for

19
00:01:23,285 --> 00:01:25,622
work that no ref and no reflog names.

20
00:01:25,962 --> 00:01:30,918
One remark about the demonstrations, said
once. The lab clock is fixed in the past

21
00:01:30,958 --> 00:01:35,731
and the expiry code reads the real clock,
so the lab configuration sets the two

22
00:01:35,771 --> 00:01:40,057
reflog periods to never. The 90 and
30 days are stated from the manual.

23
00:01:40,097 --> 00:01:44,870
On screen, expiry is shown only with the
cut-off now, which doesn't depend on a

24
00:01:44,910 --> 00:01:48,587
clock. A relative cut-off would
depend on the recording date.

25
00:01:50,427 --> 00:01:53,849
state the default retention periods
and which event starts each;

26
00:01:54,189 --> 00:01:59,220
name the situations in which no reflog
exists or it is deleted at once;

27
00:01:59,560 --> 00:02:03,142
say which four commands write
ORIG_HEAD and why it is one slot;

28
00:02:03,482 --> 00:02:08,380
find lost commits with git
fsck and explain --no-reflogs;

29
00:02:08,720 --> 00:02:11,607
triage a list of dangling
commits to the one that matters.

30
00:02:13,447 --> 00:02:17,931
The defaults. Three numbers govern
recoverability in a repository with default

31
00:02:17,971 --> 00:02:18,743
configuration.

32
00:02:19,083 --> 00:02:22,083
Two facts about these numbers
are routinely misunderstood.

33
00:02:22,423 --> 00:02:27,514
First, nothing happens when the time is
up. Expiry is work done by a command: git

34
00:02:27,554 --> 00:02:32,772
gc, Git's garbage collection, git reflog
expire, or the maintenance that Git starts

35
00:02:32,812 --> 00:02:38,030
after some commands. An entry that is 100
days old is still there until one of them

36
00:02:38,070 --> 00:02:38,347
runs.

37
00:02:38,687 --> 00:02:43,541
Second, the 30-day rule is the one that
matters for recovery, because it applies to

38
00:02:43,581 --> 00:02:48,494
the entries you recover from. The manual
says such entries "are generally created as

39
00:02:48,534 --> 00:02:53,388
a result of using git commit --amend or git
rebase and are the commits prior to the

40
00:02:53,428 --> 00:02:58,282
amend or rebase occurring". The exact test,
from the source: an entry is subject to

41
00:02:58,322 --> 00:03:03,176
the shorter period if its old value or its
new value isn't reachable from the ref's

42
00:03:03,216 --> 00:03:03,884
current tip.

43
00:03:04,224 --> 00:03:09,355
The grace period isn't added on top.
gc.pruneExpire compares the cut-off with the

44
00:03:09,395 --> 00:03:14,271
modification time of the object file.
The two weeks are measured against that

45
00:03:14,311 --> 00:03:18,677
modification time, not from the
moment the object became unreachable.

46
00:03:19,017 --> 00:03:24,388
Try it now, on paper. Thirty seconds: a
commit was written 40 days ago and hasn't

47
00:03:24,428 --> 00:03:28,197
been touched since. Today it
loses its last reflog entry.

48
00:03:28,237 --> 00:03:31,337
How much grace period is
left? Say it out loud.

49
00:03:36,177 --> 00:03:40,130
Possibly none. That commit may
already be older than the cut-off.

50
00:03:40,170 --> 00:03:45,105
The grace period protects what never had
a reflog entry and was written recently.

51
00:03:45,445 --> 00:03:50,076
The exceptions. Four situations give you
less than the table promises, and one gives

52
00:03:50,116 --> 00:03:50,576
you more.

53
00:03:50,916 --> 00:03:56,337
A deleted branch loses its reflog at once.
The manual of git branch says so for -d

54
00:03:56,377 --> 00:04:01,798
and -D. The commits stay reachable from
the HEAD reflog if you ever had the branch

55
00:04:01,838 --> 00:04:07,059
checked out. The same holds for a
remote-tracking branch that git fetch --prune

56
00:04:07,099 --> 00:04:07,592
removes.

57
00:04:07,932 --> 00:04:11,532
A commit that was never checked
out has no HEAD reflog entry.

58
00:04:11,572 --> 00:04:16,486
A teammate's branch that you only fetched
exists in your clone as a remote-tracking

59
00:04:16,526 --> 00:04:21,499
ref. When that ref is pruned, nothing names
the commits, and they are on layer three

60
00:04:21,539 --> 00:04:22,513
from that moment.

61
00:04:22,853 --> 00:04:27,336
A bare repository, one with no working
tree, as on a server, keeps no reflogs by

62
00:04:27,376 --> 00:04:31,859
default. core.logAllRefUpdates is true by
default in a repository with a working

63
00:04:31,899 --> 00:04:36,552
tree and false by default in a bare repository.
A server that receives a force push

64
00:04:36,592 --> 00:04:39,831
therefore has no record of the
value that was overwritten.

65
00:04:40,171 --> 00:04:45,438
Tags, the fixed names for commits, have
no reflog. Moving a tag with git tag -f

66
00:04:45,478 --> 00:04:49,268
prints the old ID once, and
that line is the only record.

67
00:04:49,608 --> 00:04:54,581
And the one that gives you more: stash
entries, your parked uncommitted work, don't

68
00:04:54,621 --> 00:04:59,413
expire, until they are dropped. The textbook
cites a comment in Git's source for

69
00:04:59,453 --> 00:05:04,185
this, "If unconfigured, make stash never
expire", and notes that no manual page

70
00:05:04,225 --> 00:05:07,870
states it. The exemption applies
to the default periods only.

71
00:05:07,910 --> 00:05:12,581
A cut-off given on the command line
applies to every ref, refs/stash included.

72
00:05:12,921 --> 00:05:17,730
ORIG_HEAD. In one sentence: a file that
holds the commit HEAD, your current

73
00:05:17,770 --> 00:05:23,033
position, pointed at before the last
"drastic" command, so that the command can be

74
00:05:23,073 --> 00:05:28,400
undone with git reset --hard ORIG_HEAD. The
manual names four commands: git am, git

75
00:05:28,440 --> 00:05:30,275
merge, git rebase, git reset.

76
00:05:30,615 --> 00:05:35,820
git pull writes it too, because it runs a
merge or a rebase, and so does git stash

77
00:05:35,860 --> 00:05:40,618
push, through the reset it performs.
Inside .git it's one file with one ID.

78
00:05:40,658 --> 00:05:45,160
It has no reflog, and it's not a
starting point for garbage collection.

79
00:05:45,500 --> 00:05:51,642
git fsck as a search tool. In one sentence:
git fsck walks the object database from

80
00:05:51,682 --> 00:05:55,589
every starting point and
reports what it can't reach.

81
00:05:55,629 --> 00:06:00,580
🟢 SAFE, except that --lost-found
writes files into .git/lost-found.

82
00:06:00,920 --> 00:06:06,398
Four options matter. The default, --dangling,
reports unreachable objects that no

83
00:06:06,438 --> 00:06:11,917
other object refers to: the tips of lost
history, and blobs that lost their index

84
00:06:11,957 --> 00:06:15,596
entry. --unreachable reports
every unreachable object.

85
00:06:15,636 --> 00:06:19,752
--no-reflogs doesn't count reflog
entries as starting points.

86
00:06:19,792 --> 00:06:25,134
And --lost-found also writes each dangling
commit's ID and each dangling blob's

87
00:06:25,174 --> 00:06:26,429
content into files.

88
00:06:26,769 --> 00:06:31,349
The difference between "dangling" and
"unreachable" is the difference between a tip

89
00:06:31,389 --> 00:06:35,746
and a chain. On screen, the three commits
of the branch exp/hybrid from today's

90
00:06:35,786 --> 00:06:39,921
demonstration, after its deletion. If
three commits are lost, all three are

91
00:06:39,961 --> 00:06:44,596
unreachable, and only the newest is dangling,
because the newest still refers to the

92
00:06:44,636 --> 00:06:48,994
other two. For recovery you want dangling
commits: anchor the tip and the chain

93
00:06:49,034 --> 00:06:49,773
comes with it.

94
00:06:51,613 --> 00:06:56,511
Now a picture to keep. For ORIG_HEAD the
textbook's analogy is the "last number

95
00:06:56,551 --> 00:07:01,763
redial" button of a phone. It holds exactly
one number, the newest call replaces it,

96
00:07:01,803 --> 00:07:04,951
and it tells you nothing
about which call that was.

97
00:07:04,991 --> 00:07:07,014
The reflog is the full call list.

98
00:07:07,354 --> 00:07:12,208
Carry the vault from the last video for
the rest. A reflog is a ledger that is

99
00:07:12,248 --> 00:07:16,287
trimmed by a retention policy.
It's not a second copy of the ref.

100
00:07:16,327 --> 00:07:21,558
And git fsck is the audit that walks the
vault with every card and every ledger line

101
00:07:21,598 --> 00:07:26,704
in hand and lists the boxes nobody mentions.
With --no-reflogs, the auditor leaves

102
00:07:26,744 --> 00:07:29,653
the ledger at the desk
and uses the cards only.

103
00:07:29,993 --> 00:07:34,920
Where the redial button breaks as a model:
a phone's last number is at least always

104
00:07:34,960 --> 00:07:39,887
the last call. ORIG_HEAD is the position
before the last command from a short list.

105
00:07:39,927 --> 00:07:44,793
After a commit, a switch or a cherry-pick
it still holds a value from some earlier

106
00:07:44,833 --> 00:07:45,272
command.

107
00:07:47,112 --> 00:07:52,090
Stop on the third row. After day 30 the
next maintenance run removes the reflog

108
00:07:52,130 --> 00:07:57,299
entries, and because the object file is
already older than two weeks, the same run

109
00:07:57,339 --> 00:08:00,856
may delete the object. There's
no second waiting period.

110
00:08:01,196 --> 00:08:04,339
Three commits in a chain.
All three are unreachable.

111
00:08:04,379 --> 00:08:09,481
Only 2ae0c97 is dangling. The blob on the
right is both: it was staged, and then its

112
00:08:09,521 --> 00:08:10,828
index entry was reset.

113
00:08:15,668 --> 00:08:19,989
Here's that branch as a picture: three
commits, an amend, one more commit, and a

114
00:08:20,029 --> 00:08:22,714
reset. Two commits end up
off the current history.

115
00:08:23,054 --> 00:08:27,633
Predict: of six entries, how
many fall under the 30-day rule?

116
00:08:27,673 --> 00:08:29,677
Say it out loud. I'll wait.

117
00:08:34,517 --> 00:08:39,533
Four of six. The commit before the amend,
the commit that was reset away, and also

118
00:08:39,573 --> 00:08:42,985
the two entries whose other
end is one of those commits.

119
00:08:43,025 --> 00:08:47,055
Despite the word prune in the
output, the dry run removed nothing.

120
00:08:52,895 --> 00:08:57,350
The HEAD reflog wasn't named in that
command and still has every entry.

121
00:08:57,390 --> 00:09:01,086
In a real repository both logs
are trimmed in the same run.

122
00:09:01,426 --> 00:09:03,345
Now the stash. Two entries.

123
00:09:03,685 --> 00:09:08,883
The stash list is the reflog of refs/stash.
Predict what git stash list prints after

124
00:09:08,923 --> 00:09:11,377
a cut-off of now is applied to all refs.

125
00:09:11,717 --> 00:09:16,547
Nothing. The ref still points
at 71ed40a, the newest entry.

126
00:09:16,587 --> 00:09:20,756
The older entry, 89fc6b8,
is now a dangling commit.

127
00:09:21,096 --> 00:09:24,205
And the two commits from the
picture are in that list as well.

128
00:09:24,545 --> 00:09:28,610
The root cause: only the newest
stash entry is held by a ref.

129
00:09:28,650 --> 00:09:32,849
Every older entry is held by a
reflog line and by nothing else.

130
00:09:33,189 --> 00:09:37,973
The newest entry comes back. You recover
an older one in the video on uncommitted

131
00:09:38,013 --> 00:09:38,271
work.

132
00:09:38,611 --> 00:09:41,320
The server. A push into a bare repository.

133
00:09:41,660 --> 00:09:45,583
No reflogs on the bare side.
The setting isn't set there.

134
00:09:45,623 --> 00:09:49,406
Whoever administers a bare
repository can switch it on.

135
00:09:49,446 --> 00:09:53,368
git config set and git config
get need Git 2.46 or later.

136
00:09:53,708 --> 00:09:58,681
The log has one line, and main@{1} still
resolves: Git takes the old value recorded

137
00:09:58,721 --> 00:10:02,365
in that line. This is a setting
for servers you run yourself.

138
00:10:02,405 --> 00:10:07,196
A hosting service has its own instruments,
which come at the end of this module.

139
00:10:07,536 --> 00:10:12,996
An amend, two switches, a cherry-pick and
a revert: five movements of HEAD, each

140
00:10:13,036 --> 00:10:18,703
with a reflog entry, and no ORIG_HEAD. A
reset, a merge and a rebase each write it.

141
00:10:27,043 --> 00:10:32,214
Each time the value is the tip from
immediately before the command.

142
00:10:32,254 --> 00:10:38,124
Now the trap. You switch to main and
cherry-pick. Quick quiz, three options.

143
00:10:38,164 --> 00:10:44,190
Does ORIG_HEAD name the tip of main before
the cherry-pick, the old tip of the

144
00:10:44,230 --> 00:10:47,612
feature branch, or
nothing? Say it out loud.

145
00:10:52,452 --> 00:10:57,964
Option two. 68e6fff, a commit of the feature
branch from before its rebase, which no

146
00:10:58,004 --> 00:10:59,617
branch contains any more.

147
00:10:59,956 --> 00:11:05,125
git reset --hard ORIG_HEAD at this moment
would move main onto abandoned history.

148
00:11:05,165 --> 00:11:09,883
On the picture, ORIG_HEAD sits on a
dashed commit that no branch contains.

149
00:11:09,923 --> 00:11:12,519
Almost everyone trusts it once too often.

150
00:11:23,359 --> 00:11:27,877
Predict: does git fsck report the
three commits? Say it out loud.

151
00:11:32,717 --> 00:11:37,534
No. Only the blob, the content of one file.
The three commits are still reachable

152
00:11:37,574 --> 00:11:42,151
from the HEAD reflog, because the branch
was checked out when they were made.

153
00:11:42,191 --> 00:11:45,689
That default answers "what is
at risk of being collected?".

154
00:11:45,729 --> 00:11:49,526
To ask "what is no longer on any
branch?", take the reflogs out.

155
00:11:49,866 --> 00:11:54,986
One dangling commit, 2ae0c97, stands for
three unreachable commits with their trees

156
00:11:55,026 --> 00:11:55,607
and blobs.

157
00:11:55,947 --> 00:12:00,959
In a repository that has been used for
months the list of dangling commits is long:

158
00:12:00,999 --> 00:12:04,671
every amend, every rebase and
every dropped stash leaves one.

159
00:12:04,711 --> 00:12:07,227
Turn the list into something you can read.

160
00:12:07,567 --> 00:12:12,836
Date, author, subject for each tip. The
second command shows the whole lost line:

161
00:12:12,876 --> 00:12:16,310
commits reachable from the
found tip and from no ref.

162
00:12:16,650 --> 00:12:22,445
--lost-found saves the findings as files.
That's convenient for blobs: a blob has no

163
00:12:22,485 --> 00:12:26,543
name and no date and can only
be recognised by its content.

164
00:12:26,883 --> 00:12:31,114
A found commit is still unreachable.
Reading it doesn't protect it.

165
00:12:31,154 --> 00:12:36,214
Give it a ref. 🟢 SAFE: git branch <name>
<id> writes one ref and changes nothing

166
00:12:36,254 --> 00:12:36,533
else.

167
00:12:41,673 --> 00:12:46,465
From this moment the three commits are on
layer one, and no expiry or collection can

168
00:12:46,505 --> 00:12:50,434
touch them. One label on the tip,
and the whole chain is solid again.

169
00:12:52,274 --> 00:12:53,922
Five mistakes to watch for.

170
00:12:54,262 --> 00:12:58,974
"30 days in the reflog, then two more
weeks." Root cause: the prune cut-off is

171
00:12:59,014 --> 00:13:03,788
compared with the modification time of
the object file, not with the moment the

172
00:13:03,828 --> 00:13:05,372
object became unreachable.

173
00:13:05,712 --> 00:13:09,525
Expecting a deleted branch's
reflog to still exist.

174
00:13:09,565 --> 00:13:15,644
Root cause: git branch -d and -D delete
the reflog with the branch; only the HEAD

175
00:13:15,684 --> 00:13:18,742
reflog, or git fsck, still knows the tip.

176
00:13:19,082 --> 00:13:24,147
git reset --hard ORIG_HEAD some commands
later. Root cause: ORIG_HEAD is one slot

177
00:13:24,187 --> 00:13:29,379
written by a short list of commands, and
it keeps the value of an earlier one after

178
00:13:29,419 --> 00:13:31,270
commands that do not write it.

179
00:13:31,610 --> 00:13:37,298
"git fsck shows nothing, so the commits
are gone." Root cause: by default reflog

180
00:13:37,338 --> 00:13:42,882
entries count as starting points, so
commits that a reflog still names are not

181
00:13:42,922 --> 00:13:46,534
reported; --no-reflogs
removes them from the roots.

182
00:13:46,874 --> 00:13:51,863
Reading "dangling" as damage. Root cause:
dangling is information; only lines that

183
00:13:51,903 --> 00:13:56,524
begin with missing, broken or error
describe a repository that needs repair.

184
00:13:58,364 --> 00:14:03,275
Now, out of the lab. An ML engineer cleans
up a clone that holds six months of

185
00:14:03,315 --> 00:14:08,416
experiments. A blog post recommends expiring
all reflogs with a cut-off of now to

186
00:14:08,456 --> 00:14:12,224
"shrink the repository". Afterwards
git stash list is empty.

187
00:14:12,264 --> 00:14:15,144
It held four parked
prompt-tuning experiments.

188
00:14:15,484 --> 00:14:20,852
The lead reads the state before anyone
types more. refs/stash still points at the

189
00:14:20,892 --> 00:14:24,991
newest entry, so one experiment
comes back with git stash pop.

190
00:14:25,031 --> 00:14:30,332
The other three were held by reflog lines
only. They're dangling commits now, on

191
00:14:30,372 --> 00:14:35,673
layer three, and nothing has been pruned
yet. The team finds them with git fsck,

192
00:14:35,713 --> 00:14:41,281
anchors each one, and writes down the rule
that the root-cause box gives: long-lived

193
00:14:41,321 --> 00:14:46,822
work goes on a branch, not into the stash,
and nobody expires reflogs with --all in

194
00:14:46,862 --> 00:14:48,758
a repository that holds work.

195
00:14:50,598 --> 00:14:57,366
Your turn. Do Exercise 12.3, Level
1, "git fsck as a search tool", in

196
00:14:57,406 --> 00:15:01,707
exercises/m11-m15-investigation-recovery.md.

197
00:15:02,047 --> 00:15:07,063
Before each git fsck invocation, predict
how many dangling commits and how many

198
00:15:07,103 --> 00:15:11,862
dangling blobs it will print, and say
which starting points you included or

199
00:15:11,902 --> 00:15:16,854
excluded. Then anchor what you find and
predict what the next git fsck will no

200
00:15:16,894 --> 00:15:17,750
longer report.

201
00:15:18,090 --> 00:15:24,040
The challenge is Exercise 12.6, Level 2,
draw the graph, "Reset, commit, rescue,

202
00:15:24,080 --> 00:15:25,987
rebase", in the same file.

203
00:15:27,827 --> 00:15:29,104
Pause and answer out loud.

204
00:15:33,944 --> 00:15:38,992
A strong answer gives three numbers with
the setting behind each, and says to which

205
00:15:39,032 --> 00:15:43,650
entries the shorter reflog period applies.
It then separates two clocks: the

206
00:15:43,690 --> 00:15:48,124
timestamp of a reflog entry, and the
modification time of an object file.

207
00:15:48,164 --> 00:15:52,966
It explains that expiry happens when a
command runs and not when a date passes.

208
00:15:53,306 --> 00:15:59,001
And it lists the cases in which the first
clock never started, because there was no

209
00:15:59,041 --> 00:16:04,598
reflog entry at all. If you can add what
the lab environment changes and why, you

210
00:16:04,638 --> 00:16:09,781
have shown that you know the difference
between the manual and a particular

211
00:16:09,821 --> 00:16:10,748
configuration.

212
00:16:12,588 --> 00:16:15,516
Let's land this. You
should now be able to say:

213
00:16:15,856 --> 00:16:20,551
By default reflog entries last 90 days,
30 days if an end is off the current

214
00:16:20,591 --> 00:16:25,474
history, and unreachable objects older
than two weeks can be pruned; none of it

215
00:16:25,514 --> 00:16:27,281
happens until a command runs.

216
00:16:27,621 --> 00:16:32,854
A deleted branch, a bare repository, a tag
and a branch that was only fetched have

217
00:16:32,894 --> 00:16:34,527
no reflog to recover from.

218
00:16:34,867 --> 00:16:40,341
ORIG_HEAD is one slot written by reset,
merge, rebase and am; I use it only as the

219
00:16:40,381 --> 00:16:43,771
very next command, and the
branch reflog otherwise.

220
00:16:44,111 --> 00:16:50,491
git fsck --no-reflogs lists what no ref
reaches; a dangling commit is the tip of a

221
00:16:50,531 --> 00:16:51,352
lost chain.

222
00:16:51,692 --> 00:16:54,721
A found commit is safe only
after I give it a ref.

223
00:16:56,561 --> 00:17:04,321
Read sections 13.4 to 13.6 of Chapter 13.
Do Exercise 12.2, Level 1, "A backup ref,

224
00:17:04,361 --> 00:17:11,744
and the one slot of ORIG_HEAD", in
exercises/m11-m15-investigation-recovery.md.

225
00:17:12,084 --> 00:17:16,429
Today you learned how long each layer
lasts, and you can now correct that

226
00:17:16,469 --> 00:17:21,474
comfortable sentence about 30 days plus two
weeks. Find a dangling commit in the lab

227
00:17:21,514 --> 00:17:26,278
shell before the next video. Next time:
the recovery method, and the first three

228
00:17:26,318 --> 00:17:30,122
